<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">De Administrando Imperio</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 950</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">De Administrando Imperio is a Greek handbook on foreign policy compiled around 950 by the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a> Porphyrogennetos for his son Romanos. It describes the peoples around the empire, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a>, Rus', <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and Hungarians, and advises on how to deal with them. It is a key source for the early history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> and the steppe peoples.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/de-administrando-imperio.jpg" alt="De Administrando Imperio" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wooofer · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Constantine_VII_Porphyrogenitus_(2).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a>, born in 905, became co-emperor in 908 but was too young to rule, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-i-lekapenos">Romanos I Lekapenos</a> took over the imperial government after being crowned co-emperor in December 920. Constantine ruled personally from January 945 until his death in November 959. A scholar-emperor, he gathered a circle of educated men who wrote three unfinished books, De Administrando Imperio, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-ceremoniis">De Ceremoniis</a> and On the Themes, and a biography of his grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-i">Basil I</a>.</p><p>De Administrando Imperio had at least one anonymous collaborator. Its date is debated: chapters 29 and 45 give dates corresponding to 948/949 and 951/952, while other scholars place its compilation after 952 and before his death. The author never gave it a title; it opens with a salutation to his son.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The work joins two earlier treatises, one on the peoples neighbouring the empire and one on its provinces, to which Constantine added his own instructions for his son Romanos II. According to its preface it has four parts: a key to foreign policy towards the northerners and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>, the diplomacy to pursue with them, a survey of the surrounding nations and a summary of recent imperial administration. Chapters 1 to 8 and 10 to 12 explain policy towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> and Turks.</p><p>Chapters 12 to 40 hold traditional accounts, mixed with legends, of how the Saracens, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, Venetians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> came to occupy their lands. Chapters 43 to 46 deal with the north-east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, and chapters 49 to 52 give instructions on taxing new provinces and on civil and naval administration. Its geographic and historical information is often confused and filled with legends, but is in essence reliable. The Greek is plain, since the emperor said he avoided lofty Atticisms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Four copies survive. Johannes Meursius published the first edition in 1611 and gave it the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> title by which it is known. Anselmo Banduri produced another in 1711, and Immanuel Bekker one in 1840. The first modern edition, by Gyula Moravcsik, with an English translation by R. J. H. Jenkins, appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> in 1949, with commentaries following in 1962. Because it was written as a manual for his successor, it is regarded as one of the most important sources for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and its neighbours.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “De Administrando Imperio” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nominally emperor from 913 and in real power from 945, he sponsored the treatise <b>De Administrando Imperio</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-15">15 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Constantine VII's <b>De Administrando Imperio</b> describes their revolt and subjugation by the general Theoktistos Bryennios in the 840s.</blockquote><a href="/state/melingoi">Melingoi</a></li></ul></section>
